> Why do we want to allow comma here? There’s no comma separated list in the macro.

I would like trailing comma support for formatting purposes. Similar to how it is possible in functions to add trailing commas even though there is a fixed number of parameters. Since the parameters of this macro can get quite long, I don't think it's unusual to have them formatted in multiple lines like this:

```rust
if matches!(
    mything,
    Some(thing) if thing < a_pretty_long_variable_name,
) {
    // ...
}
```

@matklad Especially when it comes to code formatting, it makes sense to keep things consistent. With the argument that it only makes sense when there is a need to reorder parameters, then there's likely no need to have trailing commas for functions of STD that have different types for all their parameters. But chances are many people will still add trailing commas for multiline functions just so they don't have to think about it.

If I want to format this macro, I run into problems with `rustfmt`, but what I do is just remove the `!` and then format it as a function so I don't have to think about things. That will put a `,` at the end of the second parameter, for uniformity. I then change it back to a macro and run into an error. Then when my code is reviewed, someone notices this missing uniformity and mentions that there should be a trailing comma there, just to find out that it's not possible. This is how I got aware of this "problem".

Is it functionally necessary to have a trailing comma here? I don't want to say there's no purpose at all, but the main reasons why trailing commas are allowed don't really apply here. Will it reduce the friction of writing code when using this macro? I think it does and I think that makes it worth it to add.

